Triangle $ABC$ lies in the square base and is right-angled at $B$.

$\begin{aligned}AC^2&=5^2+5^2\\AC^2&=50\\AC&=5\sqrt{2}\text{ m}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,3);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (2.75,0)--(2.75,0.25)--(3,0.25);
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$} (B) node[below right] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$5\text{ m}$} (3,1.5) node[right] {$5\text{ m}$} (1.5,1.5) node[above left] {$5\sqrt{2}\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

First find the diagonal of the square base.

$\begin{aligned}AC^2&=6^2+6^2\\AC^2&=72\\AC&=6\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

The vertical edge $CD$ is perpendicular to the base. Triangle $ACD$ is right-angled at $C$.

$\begin{aligned}AD^2&=(6\sqrt{2})^2+6^2\\AD^2&=108\\AD&=6\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
